\title{What a run of the executable does without options: the settings of a single cold run}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{platform}
\tag{performance}
\tag{gc}
\tag{benchmark}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted. \src{scripts/build.sh} bakes the collector policy into the image and \code{rlvm-main} has the scanner-thread default; \code{\verb~|rlvm --version~} prints both.}

\p{\strong{Context.} The release is [one executable](design-packaging) whose plain run must be the fastest configuration there is. What one invocation of an executable is, is a single run of a fresh process with the load pipelined and the automatic worker count, so the settings are chosen on cold single runs of the native image over the eight corpora, and not on the warm passes of [the metric](dec-7QMU); for the image the two are close, a cold run of Mathlib is within 4 \% of a warm pass. The settings in question: the worker count, the load mode and the scanner threads of the reader, which had been chosen with the reference engine and the machine's earlier switch set (see [which launcher was for what](dec-WX9Z)), and the memory settings that are baked into the image, which had never been measured: the image ran with a heap limit of 13 GB and the collector's own defaults. A profile of a Mathlib pass had charged the image 121 collections with 2.2 s of pauses and 7.7 s of kernel time on 6.3 million page faults.}

\p{\strong{Decision.}}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Engine and switch set}: not decided here. The engine is the one the engines' priorities select and the switch set is the machine's own default; the integrator of the machine publishes both, and a run without options follows.}
\li{\strong{Workers}: one per CPU, at most 8. Unchanged: 7 workers are level with 8, 6 are 5 \% slower.}
\li{\strong{Load}: checked while it is read, for a file of 8 MiB or more and two or more workers. Unchanged: a batch load is 21 \% slower.}
\li{\strong{Scanner threads of the reader}: one, for a file of 256 MiB or more. It was two.}
\li{\strong{Heap}: at most 13 GB, as before, and the collection policy \code{BySpaceAndTime} of the serial collector in place of its default, \code{Adaptive2}. No minimum heap and no bound on the young generation is baked in.}
}

\p{\strong{How it was measured.} Every figure is the wall clock of one fresh process of the CE native image, from exec to exit, with 8 workers on the 8 measurement cores of the aarch64 development host, inside a quiet-core window, while the other 12 cores were busy with builds and gates. The configurations of a comparison alternate inside each round, a cell is the median of its runs and the number of runs is given; two runs of one configuration differ by up to 5 \%, on reachability by up to 10 \%. In the screening the engine is the machine with the switch set \code{\verb~|default,eager,skipargs,congruence~} named on the command line, which became its default set two hours later; the tables of the released image are runs without any option. The memory settings were given on the command line of one image; they are run-time defaults, so baking them in changes nothing else.}

\subtree[dec-EGMG-heap]{
\title{The heap and the collector}

\p{What the page faults are. The heap of a run is mostly large primitive arrays: in a trace of the heap chunks during an Init run, 94 \% of the old generation and 85 \% of the survivor spaces, by bytes, were arrays of 128 KiB or more, each in a chunk of its own. The serial collector of GraalVM CE 25 keeps freed chunks for reuse only if they are of the standard size, 512 KiB; it returns the chunk of a large array to the system as soon as the array dies, and a new large array is new memory from the system, which faults on first touch. So the number of page faults follows the number of bytes allocated, whatever the collector is told: with a minimum heap of 6 GB the collector never ran on cedar and the run had more page faults than before, not fewer. The option that bounds the memory kept for reuse (\code{MaxHeapFree}) does not apply to these chunks and changed nothing. Larger standard chunks, which would take arrays up to 1 MiB, are only allowed together with the copying old generation, and that image was 5 \% slower in the geometric mean. What the settings can change is the number and the kind of collections.}

\p{The screening, on an image built with the defaults, each setting given on its command line:}

