\ul{
\li{\code{BySpaceAndTime} keeps a young generation of fixed size, 256 MB, and divides the collector's time evenly between young and full collections. The default policy ran this workload with an eden of about 54 MB and a full collection every 12 to 15 young ones: a Mathlib run made 184 collections, 12 of them full; with \code{BySpaceAndTime} it makes 41, 7 of them full. The gain is 1 to 6 \% of the wall clock on every corpus but reachability, whose runs scatter by more than that.}
\li{The price is memory: garbage stays longer in the old generation. Peak resident memory is 12 to 30 \% higher on the corpora, 4.0 against 5.4 GB on Mathlib. It is bounded by the heap limit, which was sized for the 16 GB of the arena's runner.}
\li{A young generation of 1 \% of the heap, 133 MB, gains the same and holds as much as the default policy on the middle corpora, but 7.2 GB on Mathlib: rejected. Given as a percentage it follows a smaller \code{\verb~|-Xmx~}; an absolute bound makes the image refuse to start under a smaller run-time heap limit, with exit status 1.}
\li{The policy's time ratio, measured with that young generation on cedar, navier-stokes-euler and cslib: 20 \% of the collector's time for young collections gives a third of the gain at the memory of the default policy; 80 \% gives no more than 50 \% and holds more.}
\li{A minimum heap of 2 or 6 GB: nothing consistent, navier-stokes-euler 9 \% slower, up to 2.3 times the resident memory. The policies \code{Proportionate} and \code{Adaptive}, one round each on five corpora: within 1.5 \% of the default.}
\li{A small input is not affected, since no setting touches memory in advance: a tutorial test peaks at 20 MB and the prelude at 48 MB before and after.}
}

