\p{The eight slowest published arena tests stress the reference engine on one thread. They were measured an hour earlier with the variant that has the young generation at 1 \% of the heap, two runs each: 0.83 (gf3-matrix-product) to 1.03 (magma-list-deep-n36) times the wall clock, 0.98 in the geometric mean, and up to 45 \% more resident memory on the tests that fill the old generation (magma-list-deep-n36 3.8 against 5.3 GB). With the policy as it is baked in, perf/app-lam ran in 11.2 s with 4.8 GB in the arena-shaped container, against 11.1 s with 4.0 GB before, when the reference engine checked it; the machine, the default engine now, checks it in 0.06 s. All of them are far from the arena's limits.}

\ul{
\li{The two changes together are worth 7 \% in the geometric mean of the eight corpora, most on the middle ones: con-leche 0.83, navier-stokes-euler 0.86 times the wall clock. Init is level within the scatter of its runs, 1.02; no corpus is slower by more than that.}
\li{\strong{Mathlib is settled}: 17.4 s before, 16.4 s now, 0.94. The two settings add: the collection policy alone gives 0.96, the one scanner thread alone 0.97. An hour earlier, on the image of the previous machine jar, a single pair of runs 20 minutes apart had said 16.2 against 16.9 s; that pair was not comparable.}
\li{The price is resident memory where the old generation holds garbage longer: Mathlib 4.1 against 5.6 GB. With the default configuration the other corpora are within 13 \% of before, in either direction.}
\li{The reference engine by name gains 5 to 6 \% on Init, Std and cedar, is level on reachability, and holds 10 to 50 \% more memory.}
}
}

